In a corded screwdriver, the cord provides a continuous power supply, but again, the control system is right there in the tool's body, so proximity isn't an issue because it's all integrated. The proximity ensures that the electrical signals can be sent and received without any loss or delay.

I don't see any scenario where these two components wouldn't need to be close together for the screwdriver to work as intended. If they were separated by a distance, even if connected via wires, it might complicate the design and potentially reduce performance or reliability. So, yes, their proximity is essential because it's integral to how the tool operates.
